{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"475cb31d664b893b1a57720386cddc7657d6127f8bcdd2c21f7e374c333ed213","slug":"pages.m05-scatterplot-2-of-5","location":"/pages/048-m05-scatterplot-2-of-5.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Scatterplot (2 of 5)","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"City College of San Francisco","given":"City College","non_dropping_particle":"of","family":"San Francisco"},"name":"City College of San Francisco","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","free":true,"CC":true}},"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"thumbnail":"/textbook/build/scatterplot5-f8a0b4a0fab0c49cf3538e28efdc28dd.gif","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"048-m05-scatterplot-2-of-5.md","url":"/textbook/build/048-m05-scatterplot--5dfa4331026affc079235d1c1a51cf97.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Learning Objectives","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"YAbqQ1CZMb"}],"key":"iSto2sjwGQ"},{"type":"code","lang":"","value":"- Graphically display the relationship between two quantitative variables and describe: a) the overall pattern, and b) striking deviations from the pattern.","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"TsxKM7GaEg"}],"key":"Z34gp304Yw"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interpreting the scatterplot","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"lYEFdD9mVz"}],"identifier":"interpreting-the-scatterplot","label":"Interpreting the scatterplot","html_id":"interpreting-the-scatterplot","implicit":true,"key":"UaXiktWsDv"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"How do we explore the relationship between two quantitative variables using the scatterplot? What should we look at, or pay attention to?","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"wqKFSVostC"}],"key":"nuAqxepzVY"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recall that when we described the distribution of a single quantitative variable with a histogram, we described the overall pattern of the distribution (shape, center, spread) and any deviations from that pattern (outliers). ","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"oYdqRU4ZxA"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We do the same thing with the scatterplot.","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"PkRG92gUVs"}],"key":"NjoF7Nu19X"},{"type":"text","value":" The following figure summarizes this point:","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"AWweIO0Luh"}],"key":"gdoMuzjbvB"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/scatterplot5-f8a0b4a0fab0c49cf3538e28efdc28dd.gif","alt":"When describing the relationship between two quantitative variables using a scatterplot, we look at: (1) The overall pattern, which can be described using direction, form, and strength. We also look at (2) Deviations from the pattern, which result from outliers.","key":"PmaRvCUExv","urlSource":"images/scatterplot5.gif"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"OHFcaIrNng"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As the figure explains, when describing the ","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"XuIbkYrIms"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"overall pattern","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"n9V6sftfv4"}],"key":"ZDCsvTsUG5"},{"type":"text","value":" of the relationship we look at its direction, form and strength.","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"ZP41Goc8Rd"}],"key":"oQL7IWKKU4"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"QaiUIVO13N"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"direction","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"SAyXNJ2oD8"}],"key":"oPHGddBJaa"},{"type":"text","value":" of the relationship can be positive, negative, or neither: A ","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"KBH5V5LcYW"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"positive (or increasing) relationship","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"zqAgSPHJXi"}],"key":"bFlpq9dcWa"},{"type":"text","value":" means that an increase in one of the variables is associated with an increase in the other. A ","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"GIaeZUXGLK"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"negative (or decreasing) relationship","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"B3WBb60MSc"}],"key":"U4RH9zXV7B"},{"type":"text","value":" means that an increase in one of the variables is associated with a decrease in the other. Not all relationships can be classified as either positive or negative.","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"bj61rjaems"}],"key":"jLwURg7SZP"}],"key":"uJnKYYvuK8"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"rkIR5RVPpf"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"form","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"xIFZTRRl4x"}],"key":"sfr39l4uBw"},{"type":"text","value":" of the relationship is its general shape. When identifying the form, we try to find the simplest way to describe the shape of the scatterplot. There are many possible forms. Here are a couple that are quite common: Relationships with a ","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"ZRNKfVTDWA"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"linear","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"jmglT965hO"}],"key":"ra9ksYPekO"},{"type":"text","value":" form are most simply described as points scattered about a line: Relationships with a ","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"I133lAzILR"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"curvilinear","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"RRF2j7Uzac"}],"key":"DpIXYmAF3q"},{"type":"text","value":" form are most simply described as points dispersed around the same curved line: There are many other possible forms for the relationship between two quantitative variables, but linear and curvilinear forms are quite common and easy to identify. Another form-related pattern that we should be aware of is clusters in the data:","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"hZTPDRRiMT"}],"key":"eRoSmrDe6J"}],"key":"FQgldR77E6"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"SuDFvZYZYJ"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"strength","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"HV0Bxf8iJl"}],"key":"uKMDmmaF1N"},{"type":"text","value":" of the relationship is determined by how closely the data follow the form of the relationship. Let’s look, for example, at the following two scatterplots displaying positive, linear relationships: The strength of the relationship is determined by how closely the data points follow the form. We can see that in the top scatterplot the data points follow the linear pattern quite closely. This is an example of a strong relationship. In the bottom scatterplot, the points also follow the linear pattern, but much less closely, and therefore we can say that the relationship is weaker. In general, though, assessing the strength of a relationship just by looking at the scatterplot is quite problematic, and we need a numerical measure to help us with that. We will discuss that later in this section.","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"rWyJaAOpv3"}],"key":"dah0mhvlC5"}],"key":"uzmXKVq0ud"}],"key":"EicWs1enqd"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data points that ","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"kEMNs8qTd2"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"deviate from the pattern","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"kWZiuzirnK"}],"key":"VkSQObmWlK"},{"type":"text","value":" of the relationship are called ","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"hSLWCfvm4E"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"outliers","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"sRnxdHdKZA"}],"key":"ZrH6F3udw8"},{"type":"text","value":". We will see several examples of outliers during this section. Two outliers are illustrated in the scatterplot below:","position":{"start":{"line":23,"column":1},"end":{"line":23,"column":1}},"key":"DPHOE1T4K7"}],"key":"ei0TPGX7ye"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/scatterplot14-c29ff6221ca7409a8ee9fe419a53618f.gif","alt":"A scatterplot which has a positive relationship. Most of the points are in a line-like shape from the bottom left of the plot to the top right. However, there are two points which do not match this trend. One is far below the majority of the points and the other is far left. These points do not participate in the line-like shape at all.","key":"XI3oPkRq5g","urlSource":"images/scatterplot14.gif"}],"enumerator":"2","key":"PkTGg0l2i6"}],"key":"bmw3mpMtc5"}],"key":"UWD5KQhsn5"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Scatterplot (1 of 5)","url":"/pages/m05-scatterplot-1-of-5","group":"Section 5: Examining Relationships"},"next":{"title":"Scatterplot (3 of 5)","url":"/pages/m05-scatterplot-3-of-5","group":"Section 5: Examining Relationships"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}